== Chapter 17

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[USE TO BE MADE OF THE DOCTRINE OF PROVIDENCE.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[This chapter may conveniently be divided into two parts:—I. A general explanation is given of the doctrine of divine Providence, insofar as it serves the solid instruction and comfort of the godly, sect. 1, and in particular sect. 2-12. First, however, those are refuted who deny that the world is governed by the secret and incomprehensible counsel of God, and those also who throw the blame for all wickedness on God and absurdly claim that exercises of piety are useless, sect. 2-5. Then a holy meditation on divine Providence is added, which, in the case of prosperity, is painted to the life, sect. 6-11.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[II. An answer to two objections from passages of Scripture that attribute repentance to God and speak of something like an annulment of his decrees.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. Summary of the doctrine of divine Providence. 1. It embraces the future and the past. 2. It works by means, without means, and against means. 3. Mankind, and particularly the Church, the object of special care. 4. The way it is administered usually secret, but always just. This last point considered more fully.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. The profane denial that the world is governed by the secret counsel of God, refuted by passages of Scripture. Wholesome advice.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. This doctrine, as to the secret counsel of God in the government of the world, gives no support either to the impiety of those who throw the blame for their wickedness on God, the insolence of those who reject means, or the error of those who neglect the duties of religion.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. As regards future events, the doctrine of divine Providence not inconsistent with deliberation on man’s part.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. In regard to past events, it is absurd to argue that crimes ought not to be punished because they are in accordance with the divine decrees. 1. The wicked resist the declared will of God. 2. They are condemned by conscience. 3. The essence and guilt of the crime are in themselves, though God uses them as instruments.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. A holy meditation on divine Providence. 1. All events happen by God’s ordaining. 2. All things work for the good of the godly. 3. The hearts of men and all their endeavors are in the hand of God. 4. Providence watches over the safety of the righteous. 5. God takes special care of his elect.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. Meditation on Providence continued. 6. God in various ways curbs and defeats the enemies of the Church. 7. He overrules all created things, even Satan himself, for the good of his people.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. Meditation on Providence continued. 8. He trains the godly to patience and moderation. Examples. Joseph, Job, and David. 9. He shakes them out of their lethargy and urges them to repentance.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[9\. Meditation continued. 10. The right use of lower causes explained. 11. When the godly become negligent or imprudent in doing their duty, Providence reminds them of their fault. 12. It condemns the iniquities of the wicked. 13. It gives a right regard for the future, making the servants of God prudent, diligent, and active. 14. It makes them entrust themselves to the wisdom and omnipotence of God, and, at the same time, makes them diligent in their calling.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[10\. Meditation continued. 15. Though human life is beset with countless evils, the righteous, trusting in divine Providence, feel perfectly safe.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[11\. The use of the preceding meditation.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[12\. The second part of the chapter, answering two objections. 1. That Scripture represents God as changing his purpose, or repenting, and that therefore his Providence is not fixed. Answer to this first objection. Proof from Scripture that God cannot repent.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[13\. Why repentance is attributed to God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[14\. Second objection, that Scripture speaks of an annulment of the divine decrees. Objection answered. Answer confirmed by an example.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[Moreover,] such is the human mind’s tendency to indulge in vain subtleties that it is almost impossible for those who do not see the sound and proper use of this doctrine to avoid entangling themselves in perplexing difficulties. It will therefore be proper here to touch on the end Scripture has in view in teaching that all things are ordained by God. And it is to be observed, first, that the Providence of God is to be considered with reference both to the past and to the future; and, secondly, that in overruling all things it works at one time with means, at another without means, and at another against means. Lastly, the design of God is to show that He takes care of the whole human race, but is especially watchful in governing the Church, which he favors with a closer inspection. We must add, moreover, that although the fatherly favor and kindness of God, as well as his severity as judge, often shine out in the whole course of his Providence, yet at times, as the causes of events are hidden, the thought is apt to creep in that human affairs are whirled about by the blind impulse of Fortune; or our carnal nature tempts us to murmur, as if God were amusing himself by tossing men up and down like balls. It is true, indeed, that if we were ready to learn with calm and quiet minds, the outcome would at length make it plain that the counsel of God was in accordance with the highest reason: that his purpose was either to train his people to patience, correct their depraved desires, tame their wantonness, accustom them to self-denial, and rouse them from sluggishness; or, on the other hand, to cast down the proud, defeat the craftiness of the ungodly, and frustrate all their schemes. However much the causes may escape our notice, we must hold it certain that they are hidden with him, and so exclaim with David, “Many, O #smallcaps[Lord] my God, are Your wonderful works which You have done, and Your thoughts toward us; they cannot be reckoned up in order before You. If I would declare and speak of them, they are more than can be numbered,” (Ps 40:5). For though our adversities ought always to remind us of our sins, so that the punishment itself may move us to repentance, yet we see how Christ claims for the secret counsel of his Father a larger right than to chastise everyone as he deserves. For he says of the man who was born blind, “Neither this man nor his parents sinned, but this happened so that the works of God would be displayed in him,” (John 9:3). Here, where calamity comes even before birth, our carnal sense murmurs, as if God were unmerciful in afflicting in this way those who have not offended. But Christ declares that, if only our eyes were clear enough, we would perceive that in this spectacle the glory of his Father is brightly displayed. We must keep to modesty, not forcing God, as it were, to give an account of himself, but so revering his hidden judgments as to count his will the most just cause of all things.#footnote[“The words of Cicero apply admirably here: Nec si ego quod tu sis sequutus, non perspicio, idcirco minus existimo te nihil sine summa ratione fecisse \[Even if I do not see what you were aiming at, I do not on that account think any the less that you did nothing without the highest reason\].” (Beveridge’s note.)] When the sky is overcast with dense clouds and a violent storm arises, the darkness that meets our eyes, and the thunder that strikes our ears and stuns all our senses with terror, make us imagine that everything is thrown into confusion, though in the heavens themselves all remains quiet and serene. In the same way, when the turmoil of human affairs makes us unfit to judge, we should still hold that God, in the pure light of his justice and wisdom, keeps all these commotions in due order and guides them to their proper end. And certainly in this matter many show a monstrous madness, presuming to subject the works of God to their own calculation and to pry into his secret counsels, and to pass hasty judgment on things unknown, and that with greater license than on the doings of mortal men. What can be more preposterous than to show modesty toward our equals, choosing to suspend our judgment rather than incur the charge of rashness, while we insolently insult the hidden judgments of God, judgments that we ought to look up to and revere?

2\. No man, therefore, will rightly and usefully ponder the providence of God except the one who remembers that he has to do with his own Maker and the Maker of the world, and, with the humility that becomes him, shows both fear and reverence. This is why so many dogs today tear at this doctrine with poisoned teeth, or at least attack it with their barking: they refuse to allow God more liberty than their own reason dictates to them. With what insolence, too, they attack us for not being content with the precepts of the Law, in which the will of God is contained, and for maintaining that the world is governed by his secret counsels! As if our doctrine were a figment of our own brain, and were not everywhere distinctly declared by the Spirit and repeated in countless forms of expression! Since some feeling of shame keeps them from daring to spew out their blasphemies against heaven, they pretend to pick a quarrel with us, so that they may give freer vent to their rage. But if they refuse to admit that every event that happens in the world is governed by the incomprehensible counsel of God, let them explain for what purpose Scripture declares that “his judgments are a great deep,” (Ps 36:7). For when Moses proclaims that the will of God is not to be sought far off, in the clouds or in the deep (Deut 30:12, 13), because it was plainly set out in the law, it follows that there must be another, hidden will, which is compared to “a great deep.” It is of this will that Paul exclaims, “O, the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How unsearchable are His judgments, and untraceable His ways! Who has known the mind of the Lord? Or who has been His counselor?” (Rom 11:33, 34). It is true, indeed, that the law and the gospel contain mysteries that far surpass the measure of our perception; but since God, to enable his people to understand the mysteries he has deigned to reveal in his word, enlightens their minds with a spirit of understanding, they are no longer a deep, but a path in which they can walk safely—a lamp to guide their feet—a light of life—a school of clear and certain truth. But the wonderful way in which the world is governed is rightly called a deep, because, while it lies hidden from us, it is to be reverently adored. Moses has beautifully expressed both in a few words. “The secret things,” he says, “belong to the #smallcaps[Lord] our God, but the things revealed belong to us and to our children forever,” (Deut 29:29). We see how he bids us not only to meditate diligently on the law, but to look up with reverence to the secret Providence of God. The Book of Job also, to keep our minds humble, contains a description of this lofty theme. The author of the book, after taking a wide survey of the universe and speaking magnificently of the works of God, at length adds, “Indeed, these are but the fringes of His ways; how faint is the whisper we hear of Him!” (Job 26:14). For this reason he distinguishes in another passage between the wisdom that dwells in God and the measure of wisdom he has assigned to man (Job 28:21, 28). After speaking of the secrets of nature, he says that wisdom “is hidden from the eyes of every living thing,” and that “God understands its way.” Shortly after, he adds that it has been made known so that it might be searched out; for “He said to man, ‘Behold, the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom.’” To this the words of Augustine refer:#footnote[Augustine, #emph[Eighty-three Questions], q. 27.] “Since we do not know all the things God does concerning us in the best order, it is only in a good will that we act according to the Law; in other things we are acted upon according to the Law, since his Providence is an unchangeable Law,” (August. Quæst. lib. 83 c. 27). Therefore, since God claims for himself the right of governing the world, a right unknown to us, let this be our law of modesty and soberness: to rest in his supreme authority, regarding his will as our only rule of justice and the most just cause of all things—not, indeed, that absolute will the sophists prattle about, when by a profane and impious divorce they separate his justice from his power, but that universal overruling Providence from which nothing flows that is not right, though its reasons may be hidden from us.#footnote[See Salvian. in Tract. de Vero Judicio et Providentia Dei. Also Bernard. De Interiore Domo, cap. 25. Also Luther in Epist. ad Fratres Antwerpienses. (Beveridge’s note.)]

3\. Those who have learned this modesty will neither murmur against God over adversity in time past, nor throw the blame for their own wickedness on him, as Homer’s Agamemnon does: Ἐγὼ δ’ οὐκ αἴτιός εἰμι, ἀλλὰ Ζεὺς καὶ μοῖρα. “#emph[Do not blame me, but Jupiter and fate].” Nor, on the other hand, will they, like the young man in Plautus, destroy themselves in despair, as if swept away by the Fates: “The condition of affairs is unstable; the Fates drive men at their pleasure. I will take myself to a rock, and there end my fortune with my life.” Nor will they, following the example of another, use the name of God as a cloak for their crimes. For in another comedy Lyconides speaks like this: “God drove me to it; I believe the gods wished it. If they did not wish it, it would not be done, I know.” Rather, they will inquire and learn from Scripture what pleases God, and then, under the guidance of the Spirit, strive to attain it. Ready to follow wherever God may call, they will show by their example that nothing is more useful than the knowledge of this doctrine, which perverse men attack undeservedly because it is sometimes wickedly abused. The profane make such an uproar with their foolish absurdities that they almost, as the saying goes, confound heaven and earth. If the Lord has marked the moment of our death, it cannot be escaped; it is vain to toil and take precautions. So when one man does not dare to travel on a road he hears is infested by robbers; when another calls in the physician and wears himself out with medicines for the sake of his health; when a third abstains from coarser food so as not to injure a sickly constitution, and a fourth is afraid to live in a tumbledown house; when all, in short, devise and, with great eagerness of mind, work out ways by which they may attain what they desire—then either these are all vain remedies, seized on to change the will of God, or else his certain decree does not fix the limits of life and death, health and sickness, peace and war, and the other things that men, as they desire or hate them, strive by their own industry to obtain or avoid. Indeed, these triflers even infer that the prayers of the faithful must be perverse, not to say superfluous, since they ask the Lord to provide for things he has decreed from eternity. In short, they do away with all plans made for the future, as being contrary to the providence of God, which, without calling them to its counsel, has decreed what it would have done. And then, by ascribing whatever happens to the providence of God, they turn a blind eye to the man who is known to have deliberately planned it. Has an assassin killed an honest citizen? He has, they say, carried out the counsel of God. Has someone committed theft or adultery? Since the deed was foreseen and ordained by the Lord, he is the minister of his providence. Has a son waited unconcerned for his parent’s death, without trying any remedy? He could not resist God, who had so predetermined from eternity. So all crimes are given the name of virtues, as being in keeping with God’s ordaining.

4\. As regards future events, Solomon easily reconciles human deliberation with divine providence. For while he mocks the stupidity of those who presume to undertake anything without God, as if they were not ruled by his hand, elsewhere he speaks like this: “A man’s heart plans his course, but the #smallcaps[Lord] determines his steps,” (Prov 16:9). He means that the eternal decrees of God in no way keep us from providing for ourselves, under his will, and arranging all our affairs. And the reason for this is clear. For he who has fixed the boundaries of our life has at the same time entrusted us with the care of it, provided us with the means of preserving it, forewarned us of the dangers to which we are exposed, and supplied precautions and remedies, so that we may not be overwhelmed unawares. Now our duty is clear, namely: since the Lord has committed to us the defense of our life—to defend it; since he offers help—to use it; since he forewarns us of danger—not to rush on heedlessly; since he supplies remedies—not to neglect them. But, it is said, a danger that is not fated will not hurt us, and one that is fated cannot be resisted by any precaution. But what if dangers are not fated merely because the Lord has furnished you with the means of warding them off and overcoming them? See how far your reasoning agrees with the order of God’s dealings: you infer that danger is not to be guarded against, because, if it is not fated, you will escape without precaution; whereas the Lord bids you guard against it just because he wills it not to be fated.#footnote[Cic. de Fato. “Recte Chrysippus, tam fatale est medicum adhibere, quam convalescere \[Chrysippus says rightly: calling in a physician is as much fated as recovering\].”—See Luther on Gen 3:7, against those who abuse the doctrine of Predestination in this way. (Beveridge’s note.)] These senseless quibblers overlook what is plainly before their eyes, namely, that the Lord has given men the arts of deliberation and caution, so that they may use them in the service of his providence in preserving their life; while, on the contrary, by neglect and sloth they bring on themselves the evils he has attached to them. How is it that a prudent man, while he looks after his safety, frees himself from threatening evils, while a foolish man perishes through thoughtless rashness, unless it is that prudence and folly are, in either case, instruments of God’s ordering? God has chosen to hide all future events from us, so that we may meet them as uncertain and never stop applying the remedies provided, until they have either been overcome or have proved too much for all our care. That is why I observed earlier that the Providence of God does not always meet us in its naked form, but that God in a manner clothes it with the means he employs.

5\. The same people refer past events wrongly and thoughtlessly to bare providence. As everything that happens depends on it, therefore neither thefts, nor adulteries, nor murders are committed without the will of God intervening. Why then, they ask, should the thief be punished for robbing a man whom the Lord chose to chastise with poverty? Why should the murderer be punished for killing a man whose life the Lord had ended? If all such persons serve the will of God, why should they be punished? I deny that they serve the will of God. For we cannot say that a man who is carried away by a wicked mind is rendering service at God’s command, when he is only following his own malicious desires. The man who obeys God is the one who, being taught his will, hurries where God calls him. But how are we so taught, except by his word? The will declared in his word is therefore what we must keep in view when we act. God requires of us nothing but what he commands. If we plan anything contrary to his command, it is not obedience, but defiance and transgression. But if he did not will it, we could not do it. I admit this. But do we do evil in order to obey him? This he certainly does not command. No, rather we rush on, not thinking of what he wishes, but so inflamed by our own unbridled lust that we deliberately strive against him. And in this way, while acting wickedly, we serve his righteous ordaining, since in his boundless wisdom he knows well how to use bad instruments for good purposes. And see how absurd this way of arguing is. They insist that crimes ought not to be punished in those who commit them, because they are not committed without God’s ordering. I grant more: that thieves and murderers and other evildoers are instruments of divine Providence, used by the Lord himself to carry out the judgments he has resolved to inflict. But I deny that this gives them any excuse for their misdeeds. How could it? Will they involve God in the same iniquity as themselves, or will they cover their depravity with his righteousness? They cannot clear themselves, for their own conscience condemns them; they cannot accuse God, since they find the whole wickedness in themselves, and nothing in Him but the lawful use of their wickedness. But, it is said, he works through them. And where, I ask, does the fetid stench of a dead body come from, when it has been left uncovered and has rotted in the sun’s heat? All see that it is drawn out by the rays of the sun, but no man says on that account that the stench is in them. In the same way, since the matter and guilt of the wickedness belong to the wicked man, why should it be thought that God contracts any impurity in using it as his instrument at his pleasure? Away, then, with that dog-like insolence, which may indeed bay at the justice of God from a distance, but cannot reach it!

6\. These slanders, or rather frenzied dreams, will easily be dispelled by a pure and holy meditation on divine Providence, such meditation as piety prescribes, so that we may draw from it the best and sweetest fruit. The Christian, then, being most fully persuaded that all things come to pass by God’s ordering and that nothing happens by chance, will always turn his eyes to him as the principal cause of events, while paying due regard to lower causes in their own place. Next, he will not doubt that a special providence is keeping watch for his preservation, which will not allow anything to happen that does not turn to his good and safety. But as he has to do first with men and then with other created things, he will be sure that the providence of God reigns over both. As for men, good and bad alike, he will acknowledge that their plans, wishes, aims and powers are so under God’s hand that God has full power to turn them in whatever direction he pleases and to restrain them as often as he pleases. That a special providence watches over the safety of believers is attested by a great many very clear promises.#footnote[Ps 55:23; 1 Pet 5:7; Ps 91:1; Zech 2:8; Isa 26:1; 49:15] “Cast your burden upon the #smallcaps[Lord] and He will sustain you; He will never let the righteous be shaken.” “Cast all your#super[+] anxiety on Him, because He cares for you#super[+].” “He who dwells in the shelter of the Most High will abide in the shadow of the Almighty.” “Whoever touches you#super[+] touches the apple of my eye.” “I will be your shield, a wall of bronze; I will be an adversary to your adversaries.” “Can a woman forget her nursing child, or lack compassion for the son of her womb? Even if she could forget, I will not forget you!” Indeed, the chief aim of the historical books of Scripture is to show that the Lord guards the ways of his saints so carefully that they do not even strike their foot against a stone. Therefore, as a little earlier we rightly rejected the opinion of those who imagine a universal providence that does not stoop to take special care of each created thing, so it is of the highest importance that we recognize this special care toward ourselves. So our Savior, after declaring that not even a sparrow falls to the ground without the will of his Father (Matt 10:29, 30), at once applies it: since we are worth more than many sparrows, we ought to consider that God provides for us with all the closer care. He even extends this so far as to assure us that the hairs of our head are all numbered. What more can we wish for, if not even one hair of our head can fall except by his will? I am not speaking only of the human race in general. Since God has chosen the Church for his dwelling, there can be no doubt that in governing it he gives special proofs of his fatherly care.

7\. Confirmed by these promises and examples, the servant of God will add the passages that teach that all men are under God’s power, whether to win over their minds or to curb their wickedness and keep it from doing harm. For it is the Lord who gives us favor, not only with those who wish us well, but also in the eyes of the Egyptians (Exod 3:21), and who in various ways defeats the malice of our enemies. Sometimes he takes away all their presence of mind, so that they cannot undertake anything soundly or soberly. In this way he sends Satan to be a lie in the mouths of all the prophets in order to deceive Ahab (1 Kgs 22:22); by the counsel of the young men he makes Rehoboam so foolish that his folly deprives him of his kingdom (1 Kgs 12:10, 15). Sometimes, when he leaves them their understanding, he so fills them with terror and dismay that they can neither will nor plan to carry out what they had intended. Sometimes, too, after permitting them to attempt what lust and rage suggested, he cuts them off in their course at the right moment and does not allow them to finish what they had begun. Thus the counsel of Ahithophel, which would have been fatal to David, was thwarted before its time (2 Sam 17:7, 14). Thus, for the good and safety of his people, he overrules all created things, even the devil himself, who, as we see, dared not attempt anything against Job without God’s permission and command (Job 1:12). This knowledge is necessarily followed by gratitude in prosperity, patience in adversity, and an incredible peace of mind for the time to come. Everything, therefore, that turns out well and as he wished, the Christian will ascribe entirely to God, whether he has experienced God’s kindness through the agency of men or been helped by inanimate things. For he will reason with himself like this: Certainly it was the Lord who inclined the minds of these people in my favor, binding them to me so as to make them the instruments of his kindness. In an abundant harvest he will think that it is the Lord who hears the heavens, that the heavens may hear the earth, and the earth in turn its own offspring; in other things he will not doubt that it is the blessing of God alone that makes all things prosper, and, reminded by so many causes, he will find it impossible to be ungrateful.

8\. If anything adverse happens to him, he will at once lift his mind to God, whose hand is most effectual in impressing patience and calm moderation of mind upon us. If Joseph had kept his thoughts fixed on the treachery of his brothers, he could never have recovered a brother’s love for them. But turning toward the Lord, he forgot the wrong, and was so inclined to gentleness and mercy that he even comforts his brothers of his own accord, telling them, “It was not you#super[+] who sold me into Egypt, but by the will of God I was sent before you#super[+], to preserve your#super[+] life.” “As for you#super[+], what you#super[+] intended against me for evil, God intended for good,” (Gen 45:8; 50:20). If Job had turned to the Chaldeans, by whom he was plundered, he would instantly have been fired with revenge; but recognizing the work of the Lord, he comforts himself with this most beautiful saying: “The #smallcaps[Lord] gave, and the #smallcaps[Lord] has taken away. Blessed be the name of the #smallcaps[Lord],” (Job 1:21). So when David was attacked by Shimei with stones and curses, if he had at once fixed his eyes on the man, he would have urged his men to repay the injury; but seeing that Shimei does not act without an impulse from the Lord, he calms them instead. “So let him curse,” he says, “because the #smallcaps[Lord] has told him, ‘Curse David,’” (2 Sam 16:10). With the same bridle he elsewhere curbs the excess of his grief: “I have become mute; I do not open my mouth because of what You have done,” (Ps 39:9). If there is no more effectual remedy for anger and impatience, the man has certainly made no small progress who has learned so to meditate on divine Providence that he can always bring his mind back to this: The Lord willed it; therefore it must be borne—not only because it is unlawful to strive against him, but because he wills nothing that is not just and fitting. It all comes to this. When we are unjustly attacked by men, let us pass over their malice (which could only make our grief worse and sharpen our minds for revenge), remember to rise up to God, and learn to hold it for certain that whatever an enemy wickedly did against us was permitted and sent by his righteous ordering. Paul, to restrain our desire to repay injuries, wisely reminds us that we do not wrestle with flesh and blood, but with our spiritual enemy the devil, so that we may prepare for the contest (Eph 6:12). But to calm all the impulses of anger, the most useful thing to consider is that God arms the devil, as well as all the wicked, for the conflict, and sits as umpire, so that he may exercise our patience. But if the disasters and miseries that press on us come without the agency of men, let us call to mind the teaching of the Law (Deut 28:1), that all prosperity has its source in the blessing of God, and that all adversity is his curse. And let us tremble at that dreadful warning: “If you#super[+] walk at random against me, I also will walk at random against you#super[+],” (Lev 26:23, 24). These words condemn our sluggishness when, following our carnal sense, we think that whatever happens, either way, is a matter of chance, and so are neither moved by the kindness of God to worship him, nor stirred by his scourge to repentance. And this is why Jeremiah (Lam 3:38) and Amos (Amos 3:6) bitterly rebuked the Jews for not believing that good as well as evil was produced by the command of God. To the same effect are the words in Isaiah: “I form the light and create the darkness; I make peace and create evil. I, the #smallcaps[Lord], do all these things,” (Isa 45:7).

9\. At the same time, the Christian will not overlook lower causes. For while he regards those who do him good as ministers of God’s goodness, he will not on that account pass them by, as if their kindness deserved no gratitude; rather, feeling sincerely obliged to them, he will gladly acknowledge the obligation and try, as far as he is able, to repay it. In short, in the blessings he receives he will revere and praise God as the principal author, but will also honor men as God’s ministers, and see, as is the truth, that by the will of God he is under obligation to those by whose hand God has been pleased to show him kindness. If he suffers any loss through negligence or imprudence, he will indeed believe that it was the Lord’s will that it should be so, but at the same time he will lay it to his own charge. If someone whom it was his duty to care for, but whom he has neglected, is carried off by disease, then, though he knows that the person had reached a limit beyond which it was impossible to pass, he will not on that account make light of his fault; but because he failed to do his duty faithfully toward him, he will feel as if his own guilty negligence had caused the sick man’s death. Much less, where fraud and premeditated malice have been at work in a theft or murder, will he excuse it under the pretext of divine Providence; rather, in the same crime he will distinctly see the justice of God and the iniquity of man, as each shows itself plainly. But it is in future events above all that he will take account of such lower causes. If he is not left without human help that he can use for his safety, he will count it a blessing from God; but he will not on that account be slack in taking measures, or slow in seeking the help of those whom he sees to have the means of assisting him. Regarding all the help that created things can give him as put into his hand by the Lord, he will make use of such help as the lawful instrument of divine Providence. And since he is uncertain how any business he undertakes will turn out (except that he knows that in all things the Lord will provide for his good), he will zealously aim at what he judges best for him, as far as his abilities allow. In making his plans he will not be carried away by his own notions, but will commit and entrust himself to the wisdom of God, so that under God’s guidance he may be led into the right path. However, his confidence in outward help will not be such that its presence makes him rest easy, and its absence fills him with dismay, as if he were left destitute. His mind will always be fixed on the Providence of God alone, and he will not let any consideration of present circumstances draw him away from steadily contemplating it. Thus Joab, while he acknowledges that the outcome of the battle is entirely in the hand of God, does not therefore become idle, but vigorously carries out what belongs to his own calling, and leaves it to the Lord to govern the outcome: “Be strong,” he says, “and let us fight bravely for our people and for the cities of our God. May the #smallcaps[Lord] do what is good in His sight,” (2 Sam 10:12). The same conviction, keeping us free from rashness and false confidence, will spur us to constant prayer; and at the same time, by filling our minds with good hope, it will enable us to feel safe and to defy all the dangers that surround us.

10\. Here we are forcibly reminded of the priceless happiness of a pious mind. The ills that beset human life are countless, and they present death in as many different forms. Not to look beyond ourselves: since the body is a receptacle, indeed the nurse, of a thousand diseases, a man cannot move without carrying many forms of destruction along with him. His life is in a manner interwoven with death. For what else can be said, when heat and cold bring equal danger? Then, whichever way you turn, all the things around you not only may do harm, but almost openly threaten you and seem to hold out immediate death. Go on board a ship: you are but a plank’s breadth from death. Mount a horse: one slip of a foot endangers your life. Walk along the streets: every tile on the roofs is a source of danger. If a sharp tool is in your own hand or a friend’s, the possible harm is plain. All the savage beasts you see are so many creatures armed for your destruction. Even within a high-walled garden, where everything serves delight, a serpent will sometimes lurk. Your house, constantly exposed to fire, threatens you with poverty by day, with destruction by night. Your fields, exposed to hail, mildew, drought and other harm, threaten you with barrenness, and so with famine. I say nothing of poison, treachery, robbery and open violence, some of which beset us at home, while others follow us abroad. Amid these perils, must not man be most miserable, as one who, more dead than alive, barely draws an anxious and feeble breath, just as if a drawn sword were always hanging over his neck? It may be said that these things happen seldom, or at least not always, nor to everyone, and certainly never all at once. I admit it; but since we are reminded by the example of others that they may happen to us too, and that our life is no more exempt than theirs, we cannot help fearing and dreading them as if they were about to befall us. What could you imagine more wretched than such trepidation? Add that it is something like an insult to God to say that man, the noblest of creatures, stands exposed to every blind and random stroke of fortune. Here, however, we were only speaking of the misery man would feel if he were placed under the rule of chance.

11\. But once the light of divine Providence has shone on the believer’s soul, he is relieved and set free, not only from the extreme fear and anxiety that once oppressed him, but from all care. For as he rightly shudders at the idea of chance, so he can confidently commit himself to God. This, I say, is his comfort: that his heavenly Father so holds all things in his power—so governs them at will by his nod—so regulates them by his wisdom, that nothing happens except by his appointment; and that, received into God’s favor and entrusted to the care of his angels, he can be harmed by neither fire, nor water, nor sword, except insofar as God their master is pleased to permit. For so the Psalm sings: “Surely He will deliver you from the snare of the fowler, and from the deadly plague. He will cover you with His feathers; under His wings you will find refuge; His faithfulness is a shield and rampart. You will not fear the terror of the night, nor the arrow that flies by day, nor the pestilence that stalks in the darkness, nor the calamity that destroys at noon,” and so on (Ps 91:3–6). Hence the exultant confidence of the saints: “The #smallcaps[Lord] is on my side; I will not be afraid. What can man do to me? The #smallcaps[Lord] is on my side; He is my helper.” “Though an army encamps around me, my heart will not fear.” “Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil.” (Ps 118:6; 27:3; 23:4).

How is it, I ask, that their confidence never fails, if not because, while the world seems to roll on at random, they know that God is at work everywhere, and are sure that his work will be their safety? When they are attacked by the devil and wicked men, they would of necessity lose heart at once, if they were not strengthened by remembering and meditating on Providence. But when they call to mind that the devil and the whole band of the ungodly are held in on every side by the hand of God as with a bridle, so that they can neither conceive any mischief, nor plan what they have conceived, nor, however much they may have planned, move a single finger to carry it out, except insofar as he permits, indeed, except insofar as he commands; and that they are not only bound by his fetters, but are even forced to do him service—when the godly think of all these things, they have ample sources of comfort. For as it belongs to the Lord to arm the fury of such enemies and to turn and direct it as he pleases, so it is his also to fix its measure and its end, so that they cannot break loose and run riot as they please. Upheld by this conviction, Paul, who had said in one place that his journey was hindered by Satan (1 Thess 2:18), in another resolves to undertake it if God permits (1 Cor 16:7). If he had said only that Satan was the obstacle, he might have seemed to give Satan too much power, as if he were able even to overturn the counsels of God; but now, when he makes God the one who disposes, on whose permission all journeys depend, he shows that, whatever Satan may contrive, he can accomplish nothing except insofar as God pleases to give the word. For the same reason David, considering the many turns that human life takes as it rolls and in a manner whirls around, flees to this refuge: “My times are in Your hands,” (Ps 31:15). He might have said the course of life, or #emph[time] in the singular, but by #emph[times] he meant to express that, however unstable the condition of man may be, the changes that keep taking place again and again are governed by God. So Rezin and the king of Israel, after they had joined forces for the destruction of Judah and seemed like torches lit to destroy and consume the land, are called by the prophet “smoking firebrands.” They could give off only a little smoke (Isa 7:4). So Pharaoh, when he was dreaded by all for his wealth and strength and the multitude of his troops, is compared to a sea monster, and his troops to fish; and God declares that he will take both leader and army with his hooks and drag them wherever he pleases (Ezek 29:4). In a word, not to dwell on this any longer, pay attention, and you will at once see that ignorance of Providence is the greatest of all miseries, and the knowledge of it the highest happiness.

12\. On the Providence of God, insofar as it serves the solid instruction and comfort of believers (for satisfying the curiosity of foolish men is something that cannot be done and ought not to be attempted), enough would have been said, if a few passages did not remain that seem to suggest, contrary to the view we have set out, that the counsel of God is not firm and stable, but varies with the changes of earthly affairs. First, in reference to the Providence of God, it is said that he repented of having made man (Gen 6:6) and of having raised Saul to the kingdom (1 Sam 15:11), and that he will repent of the evil he had resolved to inflict on his people as soon as he sees some change for the better in them (Jer 18:8). Secondly, his decrees are sometimes said to be annulled. Through Jonah he had proclaimed to the Ninevites, “Forty more days and Nineveh will be overthrown!” but as soon as they repented he turned to a more merciful sentence (Jonah 3:4–10). After he had announced Hezekiah’s death to him by the mouth of Isaiah, he was moved by Hezekiah’s tears and prayers to postpone it (Isa 38:1, 5; 2 Kgs 20:1, 5). From this many argue that God has not fixed human affairs by an eternal decree, but that, according to the merits of each individual and as he judges right and just, he disposes of each single year, and day, and hour. As to repentance, we must hold that it can no more exist in God than ignorance, or error, or powerlessness. If no man knowingly or willingly puts himself where he must repent, we cannot attribute repentance to God without saying either that he does not know what is going to happen, or that he cannot escape it, or that he rushes headlong and thoughtlessly into a decision and then at once regrets it. But this is so far from the meaning of the Holy Spirit that in the very mention of repentance he declares that God is not moved by any feeling of regret, that he is not a man, that he should repent. And it is to be observed that in the same chapter both things are so joined that comparing the passages admirably removes the appearance of contradiction. When it is said that God repented of having made Saul king, the change is spoken of figuratively. Shortly after, it is added, “The Strength of Israel does not lie or repent, for He is not a man, that He should repent,” (1 Sam 15:29). In these words his immutability is plainly asserted without figure. It is certain, therefore, that in the administration of human affairs God’s ordaining is perpetual and above anything like repentance. That there might be no doubt of his constancy, even his enemies are forced to bear testimony to it. For Balaam, even against his will, had to burst out with this exclamation: “God is not a man, that He should lie, or a son of man, that He should change His mind. Does He speak and not act? Does He promise and not fulfill?” (Num 23:19).

13\. What, then, is meant by the term repentance? The very same as is meant by the other forms of expression by which God is described to us in human terms. Because our weakness cannot reach his height, any description of him that we receive must be lowered to our capacity in order to be understood. And the way of lowering it is to represent him not as he really is, but as we conceive of him. Though he is beyond every feeling of disturbance, he declares that he is angry with the wicked. So, just as when we hear that God is angry, we ought not to imagine any emotion in him, but rather consider that this way of speaking is accommodated to our perception, because God appears to us like one inflamed and provoked whenever he exercises judgment; so under the term repentance we ought not to imagine anything more than a change of action, since men usually show their dissatisfaction by such a change. Hence, because every change whatever among men is meant to correct what displeases, and the correction comes from repentance, the same term applied to God simply means that his way of acting is changed. Meanwhile there is no overturning of his counsel or will, no change of his disposition. What he had foreseen, approved and decreed from eternity he carries out with unvarying constancy, however sudden the variation may seem to the eye of man.

14\. Nor does the Sacred History, when it relates that the destruction proclaimed to the Ninevites was remitted, and that Hezekiah’s life was prolonged after his death had been announced to him, imply that the decrees of God were annulled. Those who think so are deceived about the meaning of #emph[threats], which, though they are stated simply, nevertheless contain a tacit condition, as is understood from their very purpose. Why did the Lord send Jonah to the Ninevites to predict the overthrow of their city? Why did he announce his death to Hezekiah through Isaiah? He might have destroyed both them and him without a message to announce the disaster. He had something else in view than to give them a warning of death that would let them see it from a distance before it came. It was because he wanted them not destroyed but reformed, and so saved from destruction. When Jonah prophesies that in forty days Nineveh will be overthrown, he does it in order to prevent the overthrow. When Hezekiah is forbidden to hope for longer life, it is so that he may obtain longer life. Who does not now see that by threats of this kind God wished to rouse to repentance those he terrified, so that they might escape the judgment their sins deserved? If this is so, the very nature of the case obliges us to supply a tacit condition in a plain threat. This is confirmed, too, by similar cases. The Lord, rebuking King Abimelech for having carried off Abraham’s wife, uses these words: “You are as good as dead because of the woman you have taken, for she is a married woman.” But after Abimelech has excused himself, he speaks like this: “Now return the man’s wife, for he is a prophet; he will pray for you and you will live. But if you do not restore her, be aware that you will surely die—you and all who belong to you,” (Gen 20:3, 7). You see that by the first announcement he strikes Abimelech’s mind forcibly, so as to make him eager to make amends, and that by the second he clearly explains his will. Since the other passages may be explained in the same way, you must not infer from them that the Lord took anything away from his former counsel because he revoked what he had proclaimed. When, by threatening punishment, he urges to repentance those whom he wishes to spare, he is paving the way for his eternal decree, instead of altering it one bit, either in will or in language. The only difference is that he does not express in so many words what is easily understood. The words of Isaiah must remain true: “The #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts has purposed, and who can annul it? His hand is outstretched, so who can turn it back?” (Isa 14:27).
